'''alruna''' (al-rö′nạ̈), ''n.''; pl. ''alrunæ'' (-nē). [ML., also ''alrauna'', < OHG. ''alruna'' (MHG. ''alrune'', G. ''alraun'', ''alrun'', mandrake (''alraun-bilder'', mandrake images), = D. ''alruin'' = Sw. ''alrun'', ''alruna'' = Dan. ''alrune''), mandrake; appar., as in popular apprehension, < ''al-'' (= E. ''all'') + ''runa'', Goth. ''rūna'', etc., mystery, the mandrake being an object of superstition: see ''rune'' and ''mandrake''.] '''1.''' A prophetess among the ancient Germans, regarded as similar to the druidess among the Gauls.—'''2.''' A small image carved from the root of a tree or from mandrakes, representing rudely the human figure, generally the female. {{smaller|Such images were venerated as household gods in the ancient religions of some northern peoples, the worship of them forming a special feature of certain superstitious rites. They are supposed by some to represent female magicians or druidesses. ''Brande.''}}

'''als†''', ''adv.'' and ''conj.'' An old form of ''also'' and ''as''.
{{smaller block|<poem>Better is then the lowly playne,
''Als'' for thy flocke and thee.</poem>
{{right|''Spenser'', Shep. Cal., July.}}
''Als'' longe as owre lyf lasteth lyue we togideres.
{{right|''Piers Plowman'' (B), iv. 195.}}}}

'''Alsace gum.''' Same as ''dextrine''.

